## 1. Sizing Elements

### 1.1 Intrinsic Size

Some elements have a natural size set by default, we call it **Intrinsic Size**.

<WebCodePlayground>

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
  <head> </head>
  <body>
    <img
      src="https://res.cloudinary.com/dfxicv9iv/image/upload/c_scale,h_300,w_600/v1613576396/hand-holding-tree-img_auama0.jpg"
    />
    <p class="paragraph">Once upon a time, there was a tortoise and a hare</p>
  </body>
</html>
```

```css
@import url("https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Bree+Serif&family=Caveat:wght@400;700&family=Lobster&family=Monoton&family=Open+Sans:ital,wght@0,400;0,700;1,400;1,700&family=Playfair+Display+SC:ital,wght@0,400;0,700;1,700&family=Playfair+Display:ital,wght@0,400;0,700;1,700&family=Roboto:ital,wght@0,400;0,700;1,400;1,700&family=Source+Sans+Pro:ital,wght@0,400;0,700;1,700&family=Work+Sans:ital,wght@0,400;0,700;1,700&display=swap");

.paragraph {
  color: #666666;
  padding: 4px;
  font-size: 18px;
  border: 2px solid blue;
}
```

</WebCodePlayground>

In the example above, the default sizes are applied to the image and paragraph elements.

### 1.2 Extrinsic Size

If we set a Specific Size to an element, we call an **Extrinsic Size**.

<WebCodePlayground>

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
  <head> </head>
  <body>
    <img
      src="https://assets.ccbp.in/frontend/intermediate-rwd/hand-holding-tree-img.png"
      class="image"
    />
    <p class="paragraph">Once upon a time, there was a tortoise and a hare</p>
  </body>
</html>
```

```css
@import url("https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Bree+Serif&family=Caveat:wght@400;700&family=Lobster&family=Monoton&family=Open+Sans:ital,wght@0,400;0,700;1,400;1,700&family=Playfair+Display+SC:ital,wght@0,400;0,700;1,700&family=Playfair+Display:ital,wght@0,400;0,700;1,700&family=Roboto:ital,wght@0,400;0,700;1,400;1,700&family=Source+Sans+Pro:ital,wght@0,400;0,700;1,700&family=Work+Sans:ital,wght@0,400;0,700;1,700&display=swap");

.image {
  height: 150px;
  width: 300px;
}
.paragraph {
  color: #666666;
  padding: 4px;
  font-size: 18px;
  border: 2px solid blue;
  height: 150px;
  width: 150px;
}
```
</WebCodePlayground>

In the example above, a specific size was given to the image and paragraph elements.




## 2. Handling Overflow

### 2.1 CSS Overflow Property

Content overflow can be handled using the CSS `overflow` property.

The overflow property has the following values:

1. `visible`(default) - CSS tries to avoid data loss. Hence, the `overflow: visible;` is the default value for it.
2. `hidden`- The overflow is clipped, and the rest of the content will be invisible.
3. `scroll`- The overflow is clipped, and a scrollbar is added to see the rest of the content.
4. `auto`- It is similar to `scroll`, but it adds scrollbars only when necessary.

Try out CSS `overflow` property with different values in the below Code Playground.

<WebCodePlayground>

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
  <head></head>
  <body>
    <p class="paragraph">
      Once upon a time, there was a tortoise and a hare. The tortoise was
      friendly and quiet. He did everything slowly. The hare was fast and rude.
      He did everything quickly. One day, the hare saw the tortoise and was so
      amused by it that he called him 'lazy'.
    </p>
  </body>
</html>
```

```css
@import url("https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Bree+Serif&family=Caveat:wght@400;700&family=Lobster&family=Monoton&family=Open+Sans:ital,wght@0,400;0,700;1,400;1,700&family=Playfair+Display+SC:ital,wght@0,400;0,700;1,700&family=Playfair+Display:ital,wght@0,400;0,700;1,700&family=Roboto:ital,wght@0,400;0,700;1,400;1,700&family=Source+Sans+Pro:ital,wght@0,400;0,700;1,700&family=Work+Sans:ital,wght@0,400;0,700;1,700&display=swap");

.paragraph {
  border: 2px solid blue;
  height: 150px;
  width: 180px;
  overflow: auto;
}
```

</WebCodePlayground>

### 2.2 overflow-x & overflow-y

There are two other CSS properties similar to `overflow`, which is used for handling in any one particular direction.

 - `overflow-x`- To handle overflow in the horizontal direction.
 - `overflow-y`- To handle overflow in the vertical direction.

<WebCodePlayground>

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
  <head></head>
  <body>
    <div class="image-container">
      <img
        src="https://assets.ccbp.in/frontend/intermediate-rwd/hand-holding-tree-img.png"
        class="image"
      />
    </div>
    <div>
      <p class="paragraph">
        Once upon a time, there was a tortoise and a hare. The tortoise was
        friendly and quiet. He did everything slowly. The hare was fast and
        rude. He did everything quickly. One day, the hare saw the tortoise and
        was so amused by it that he called him 'lazy'.
      </p>
    </div>
  </body>
</html>
```

```css
@import url("https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Bree+Serif&family=Caveat:wght@400;700&family=Lobster&family=Monoton&family=Open+Sans:ital,wght@0,400;0,700;1,400;1,700&family=Playfair+Display+SC:ital,wght@0,400;0,700;1,700&family=Playfair+Display:ital,wght@0,400;0,700;1,700&family=Roboto:ital,wght@0,400;0,700;1,400;1,700&family=Source+Sans+Pro:ital,wght@0,400;0,700;1,700&family=Work+Sans:ital,wght@0,400;0,700;1,700&display=swap");

.image-container {
  border: 2px solid blue;
  width: 200px;
  height: 140px;
  overflow-x: scroll;
}
.image {
  width: 300px;
  height: 120px;
}
.paragraph {
  border: 2px solid blue;
  height: 150px;
  width: 180px;
  overflow-y: scroll;
}
```

</WebCodePlayground>




## 3. Min & Max Sizes

### 3.1 Min size

The `min-height` & `min-width` CSS properties can be used to define the minimum sizes of an element.

```css
.paragraph {
  min-height: 150px;
}
```

### 3.2 Max size

The `max-height` & `max-width` CSS properties can be used to restrict the sizes of an element.

```css
.paragraph {
  min-height: 150px;
  max-height: 180px;
}
```

### 3.3 Min & Max Sizes with Overflow

If the content needs a larger than maximum height, the overflowing of content can be observed.

<WebCodePlayground>

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
  <head></head>
  <body>
    <p class="paragraph">
      Once upon a time, there was a tortoise and a hare. The tortoise was
      friendly and quiet. He did everything slowly. The hare was fast and rude.
      He did everything quickly. One day, the hare saw the tortoise and was so
      amused by it that he called him 'lazy'.
    </p>
  </body>
</html>
```

```css
@import url("https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Bree+Serif&family=Caveat:wght@400;700&family=Lobster&family=Monoton&family=Open+Sans:ital,wght@0,400;0,700;1,400;1,700&family=Playfair+Display+SC:ital,wght@0,400;0,700;1,700&family=Playfair+Display:ital,wght@0,400;0,700;1,700&family=Roboto:ital,wght@0,400;0,700;1,400;1,700&family=Source+Sans+Pro:ital,wght@0,400;0,700;1,700&family=Work+Sans:ital,wght@0,400;0,700;1,700&display=swap");

.paragraph {
  border: 2px solid blue;
  width: 150px;
  min-height: 150px;
  max-height: 180px;
  overflow: auto;
}
```

</WebCodePlayground>




